```regex
<(?!\/?(?:p|br)\b)[^>]*>
```

With global flag, e.g. in JavaScript:

```js
const stripped = html.replace(/<(?!\/?(?:p|br)\b)[^>]*>/gi, '');
```

Or in Python:

```python
import re
stripped = re.sub(r'<(?!</?(?:p|br)\b)[^>]*>', '', html, flags=re.I)
```

**How it works:** matches `<` not followed by `p` or `br` (with optional `/` for closing tags), then everything up to the next `>`.

**Caveats:**
- Doesn't handle `>` inside attribute values (e.g. `<div data-x="a>b">`)
- Doesn't handle comments (`<!-- -->`) or CDATA
- Regex-based HTML parsing is fragile — for real-world HTML, use a parser:

```js
// Safer: DOM-based (browser)
const doc = new DOMParser().parseFromString(html, 'text/html');
doc.body.querySelectorAll('*').forEach(el => {
  if (!['P','BR'].includes(el.tagName)) el.replaceWith(...el.childNodes);
});
const stripped = doc.body.innerHTML;
```

Yes — use a regex with word boundaries (`\b`) and `Pattern.CASE_INSENSITIVE`:

```java
import java.util.regex.*;

Pattern p = Pattern.compile("\\bhello\\b", Pattern.CASE_INSENSITIVE);
Matcher m = p.matcher("Say Hello to me");
if (m.find()) {
    System.out.println("Match found: " + m.group());
}
```

Or with `matches()` for the entire string:

```java
boolean isMatch = "HELLO".matches("(?i)\\bhello\\b");
```

The `(?i)` inline flag is a shorthand for `CASE_INSENSITIVE`.

**Notes:**
- `\b` matches word boundaries, so `"hello"` won't match inside `"helloworld"`.
- If your search word comes from user input, use `Pattern.quote()` to escape special regex characters:

```java
Pattern p = Pattern.compile("\\b" + Pattern.quote(word) + "\\b", Pattern.CASE_INSENSITIVE);
```

- For Unicode case-insensitive matching, add `Pattern.UNICODE_CASE` (or use `(?iu)`).

