JS is single-threaded. The event loop lets it handle async work:

1. Call stack runs your code
2. Async stuff (timers, fetch) goes to the browser/Node
3. When done, callbacks wait in queues
4. Event loop: "stack empty?" → pushes next callback onto the stack

**Order:** sync code → microtasks (promises) → macrotasks (setTimeout)

```js
console.log("1");
setTimeout(() => console.log("2"), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("3"));
console.log("4");
// Output: 1, 4, 3, 2
```

Promises beat setTimeout because microtasks drain fully before any macrotask runs.

