I don't see the API code in your message — paste it and I'll convert it exactly. Meanwhile, here's the standard pattern for adding promises without breaking the callback interface:

```js
function fetchUser(id, callback) {
  // Support both styles: detect callback
  const maybePromise = callback === undefined;

  const exec = (cb) => {
    return db.query('users', id, (err, user) => {
      if (err) return cb(err);
      cb(null, user);
    });
  };

  if (!maybePromise) {
    // v1 path: callback style, return value ignored
    exec(callback);
    return;
  }

  // v2 path: promise style
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    exec((err, user) => (err ? reject(err) : resolve(user)));
  });
}
```

Usage stays compatible both ways:

```js
// v1 callers — unchanged, still works
fetchUser(1, (err, user) => { ... });

// new promise callers
const user = await fetchUser(1);
```

Key points:
- Detect the callback by checking if the last arg is a function (`typeof args[args.length-1] === 'function'`) if your API has optional params.
- Never reject *and* call the callback — pick one path.
- If the underlying API already returns a promise/thunk, you can skip the wrapper and just branch.

