**JavaScript** — use a `Map` (single pass, O(n)):

```js
const unique = [...new Map(arr.map(item => [item.id, item])).values()];
```

This keeps the **last** occurrence of each id. To keep the **first** occurrence instead:

```js
const seen = new Set();
const unique = arr.filter(item => 
  seen.has(item.id) ? false : seen.add(item.id)
);
```

Both are O(n) and much faster than `filter` + `findIndex` (which is O(n²)). For very large arrays, the `Map` version is typically fastest since it's a single pass with no callback overhead beyond the map function.

CORS errors on DELETE (but not GET/POST) happen because **DELETE is not a "simple" request** — it triggers a **preflight**.

- Simple requests (GET, POST with basic content types, HEAD) go straight through and the browser just checks the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` response header.
- DELETE, PUT, PATCH, or requests with custom headers (like `Authorization` or `Content-Type: application/json`) force the browser to first send an **OPTIONS** preflight request.

Your server is likely failing one of these:

1. **Not responding to OPTIONS at all** (404/405), so the preflight fails.
2. **Not including DELETE in allowed methods:**
   ```
   Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, DELETE
   ```
3. **Not echoing the requested headers** in `Access-Control-Allow-Headers` (e.g., `Authorization`, `Content-Type`).
4. **Preflight blocked by middleware/auth** — many setups require auth on all routes, but the OPTIONS request has no credentials.

Fix example (Express):

```js
app.options('/items/:id', (req, res) => {
  res.set({
    'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': 'https://yourapp.com',
    'Access-Control-Allow-Methods': 'GET, POST, DELETE, OPTIONS',
    'Access-Control-Allow-Headers': 'Content-Type, Authorization',
  });
  res.sendStatus(204);
});
```

Check the Network tab — you'll see the failed OPTIONS request. Fix the server's response to it, and DELETE will work.

