Use function overloads or a conditional return type with a generic:

**Overloads** (simplest):

```typescript
function parse(input: string): string;
function parse(input: number): number;
function parse(input: string | number): string | number {
  return input;
}
```

**Generic with conditional return type** (maps param to result):

```typescript
type Result<T extends string> =
  T extends "json" ? object :
  T extends "text" ? string :
  never;

function fetchIt<T extends string>(type: T): Result<T> {
  // ...
}

const a = fetchIt("json"); // object
const b = fetchIt("text"); // string
```

**Lookup map** (often cleaner than nested conditionals):

```typescript
type ReturnMap = {
  json: object;
  text: string;
  blob: Blob;
};

function fetchIt<K extends keyof ReturnMap>(type: K): ReturnMap[K] {
  // ...
}
```

The lookup-map approach is usually preferred — it scales well and gives full autocompletion on the parameter.
